#X text 495 573 updated for Pd version 0.57;
#X text 21 17 Pure Data offers a "data structure" feature that allows you to visualize quantitative data \, such as a musical score \, in a way that freely reflects your own visual design. Objects in Pd allow you to access and/or modify the data - for example \, you could make a visualized audio analysis of an incoming sound \, edit it \, and then use the result to control a synthesizer.;
#X text 21 273 In this series of example patches you'll see the central idea behind Pure Data - not to make a Max-like program \, but rather to make data visible and audible for musical purposes. (But the Max stuff is necessary infrastructure and to use the features shown here you will need to understand the control and audio examples as well as this "data structures" series.);
#X text 21 130 The Pure Data features shown here are meant as a response to a dream shared by electronic musicians since the early days of audio synthesis: to be able to treat sound quality ("timbre") as a musical element that could be specified \, controlled \, and in general used for music composition in the same way as pitch and rhythm have long been. To make sound visible and editable is a grandiose project and we can't claim to have realized it yet - this is an unfinished project.;
#X text 22 384 The objects to the right \, called "scalars" \, are members of a data type (a "structure") that is defined in the "how-it-works" window below. With the patch in run mode you can click on the displayed numbers (five for each of the objects) to alter them \, or you can click on the hot point shown as a small black square to move it around. In edit mode you can select \, move \, or copy/paste the objects.;
#X text 196 501 Everything is defined in a subwindow \; you can click here and see --->;
